Entity wire has `callsign` (nullable; null for AI). Presence entries have `callsign` and `playerId` presumably. So:

Prune rule in wsClient: track per-entity callsign for player-owned entities. When a presence frame arrives, compute the set of present callsigns. For each tracked entity id whose callsign is non-null and NOT in the present set (and not our own), delete its buffer (it's a departed player's ship).

Careful: AI ships have callsigns like "AI-…" that are never in presence → they'd get pruned every presence frame. Bad. Distinguish: only prune entities that were EVER seen in the presence roster (i.e., mark entities whose callsign appeared in a presence frame as "player-backed"), then prune only those that later vanish from the roster.

Also edge: player leaves presence then rejoins → new presence includes them → but we already dropped the buffer; the 10Hz broadcast re-creates it. Fine.

Also caution: a player might be in the roster but their entity id differs between sessions — no matter.

This is a reasonably clean design: "liveness from presence".

Where to implement? wsClient already handles `presence` frames (applying to the presence store). I'll add there:

```ts
/** entity id → callsign, for entities whose callsign was ever seen
 *  in the presence roster (i.e. player-backed, not AI). */
#playerEntities = new Map<string, string>();   // entityId -> callsign
/** current roster callsigns */
#presentCallsigns = new Set<string>();
```

On each entity frame: `if (ent.callsign) { const known = this.#presentCallsigns.has(ent.callsign); ... }` — but the roster may arrive after the entity. So: record `entityCallsigns: Map<entityId, callsign>` for every entity with a non-null callsign; on presence frames, mark which of those ids are player-backed (`#playerEntities`) and prune ids whose callsign is not present.

Implementation in presence handler:
```ts
this.#presentCallsigns = new Set(payload.players.map(p => p.callsign));
// drop departed player entities
for (const [id, callsign] of this.#entityCallsigns) {
  if (!this.#playerEntities.has(id)) continue;   // not roster-backed yet
  if (this.#presentCallsigns.has(callsign)) continue;
  this.#entityCallsigns.delete(id);
  this.#playerEntities.delete(id);
  this.remoteBuffers.delete(id);
}
```

Hmm, but `#playerEntities` marking: when a presence frame arrives, for each entity id whose callsign ∈ roster → mark as player-backed. On the FIRST presence frame after join, the roster includes both A and B (both joined). Their entity ids may or may not be in `#entityCallsigns` yet (join snapshot comes before/after presence?). The join sequence: `join_system` → server sends presence then state_snapshot (or the reverse). If the entity hasn't been seen yet, marking fails, but the next presence frame... presence frames only fire on roster change. So a later-disconnect would not prune unless marking happened.

Fix: mark player-backed lazily in the entity handler too: when we see an entity whose callsign is in `#presentCallsigns`, mark it. And on presence frames, also scan existing `#entityCallsigns` for membership. Both paths mark. That covers it.

But careful about the *initial* marking problem: entity arrives before the first presence frame → not marked. Then presence arrives → the presence handler scans `#entityCallsigns` (which has the id) and marks it. Good, the scan happens at presence time.

Also: the departure check on the same presence frame would then also run — the callsign IS in the roster, so no prune. Good.

Risk: at the very first presence frame (before anyone else joined), if A's own ship entity is in `#entityCallsigns` with callsign A and roster contains A → marked player-backed, not pruned. Fine.

Risk: the departed player's callsign might linger in the roster for a frame or two? Server pushes on roster change, so it should be prompt.

Additional risk: multi-character: a player on foot has a character entity with the same callsign → also pruned. Good.

