Now improve the e2e to capture a screenshot with a remote mesh in view (freeze the rig, aim the camera at a visible remote mesh). Add that before the screenshots.

Implementation in the spec:

```ts
async function frameRemoteMesh(page: Page): Promise<string | null> {
  return page.evaluate(() => {
    const w = window as unknown as {
      __driftRenderHost?: { cameraRig: { update(dt: number): void }; camera: unknown; render(): void };
      __driftRemoteEntityLayer?: { root: { children: Array<{ visible: boolean; position: { x: number; y: number; z: number } }> } };
    };
    const host = w.__driftRenderHost;
    const layer = w.__driftRemoteEntityLayer;
    if (!host || !layer) return null;
    const target = layer.root.children.find((c) => c.visible);
    if (!target) return null;
    // Freeze the chase rig so the next render keeps our framing.
    host.cameraRig.update = () => {};
    const cam = host.camera as THREE.PerspectiveCamera;
    cam.position.set(target.position.x + 7, target.position.y + 4, target.position.z + 7);
    cam.lookAt(target.position.x, target.position.y, target.position.z);
    host.render();
    return `${target.position.x.toFixed(1)},${target.position.y.toFixed(1)},${target.position.z.toFixed(1)}`;
  });
}
```
TS in page.evaluate: `host.camera` typed as unknown → cast via `as { position: ...; lookAt(...) }`.

The screenshot after that will show the remote mesh with lighting. Since the rig is frozen, later assertions (none depend on the camera) are fine. Do this right before pageA screenshot.

Then assert the return is non-null.

Let's implement, then run e2e.

